---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ToolPageShell from '../components/astro/ToolPageShell.astro';
import Faq from '../components/astro/Faq.astro';
import EditTool from '../components/tools/edit/EditTool';
import { webAppJsonLd, faqJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can I really edit existing text?', a: 'Yes. Click any text, retype it, and export. Under the hood the original text is genuinely removed from the PDF and your replacement is written in its place, so small edits look seamless — and the old text can\'t be recovered by copy-pasting or text extraction.' },
  { q: 'Will the font match exactly?', a: 'Usually very closely. Your replacement text is written in fonts that line up letter-for-letter with the ones behind most documents (like Arial, Times New Roman, and Courier). Unusual decorative fonts are approximated.' },
  { q: 'Is my document uploaded?', a: 'No. Rendering, editing, and export all run in your browser.' },
  { q: 'Does it add a watermark?', a: 'Never. Your exported PDF contains only your document and your edits.' },
  { q: 'Can I rotate or resize pages?', a: 'Yes. Each page has rotate buttons (90° steps), and the toolbar’s page-size menu scales the whole document by percentage or fits it to A4/Letter.' },
];
const description = 'Edit text in a PDF free — no upload, no watermark, no Acrobat. Click any text and type, rotate and resize pages; everything happens locally in your browser.';
---
<Base title="Edit PDF text — free, local, no watermark | localdobe" {description} canonicalPath="/edit-pdf"
  jsonLd={[webAppJsonLd('Edit PDF', '/edit-pdf', description), faqJsonLd(faq)]}>
  <ToolPageShell h1="Edit a PDF" sub="Click any text and retype it. Add new text, rotate and resize pages. All in your browser.">
    <EditTool client:load slot="tool" />
    <h2>How to edit a PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your PDF into the box above (or click to browse).</li>
      <li>Click any existing text to edit it in place, or click <strong>+ Add text</strong> and then click the page to place a new text box.</li>
      <li>Rotate a page with the ⟲ ⟳ buttons in its corner, or scale the whole document with the page-size menu.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Export PDF</strong> and download the result.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>How in-browser PDF editing works</h2>
    <p>PDFs don't store paragraphs the way a word processor does — every letter is placed at a fixed position on the page, and there's no reflowable "text box" to type into. So when you edit a line here, localdobe deletes the original letters from the page entirely and writes your new text in their place, in a font chosen to match the original as closely as possible. Because the old text is actually removed rather than painted over, it won't show up if someone selects, copies, or searches the text of the exported PDF.</p>
    <p>In a small number of cases — scanned pages, flattened text, or unusual PDF structure — the original text can't be safely identified and removed on its own, so localdobe falls back to covering it with a solid rectangle instead, the way earlier versions of this tool always worked. When that happens on export, you'll see a note telling you how many edits used the fallback.</p>
    <p>This approach shines for the edits people actually need most: fixing a typo, updating a date or a name, filling in a blank on a form, or swapping one line of text for another. It's less suited to rewriting whole paragraphs — surrounding text won't reflow around your edit. For that kind of heavy restructuring, you'd want the original source file and a word processor.</p>
    <p>Want the details? Read <a href="/blog/edit-pdf-without-acrobat">how to edit a PDF without Acrobat</a> and <a href="/blog/free-pdf-editors-watermarks">why so many "free" PDF editors add their own watermark</a>.</p>
    <Faq items={faq} />
  </ToolPageShell>
</Base>
